#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# weierstrass_invariants

#% text
# `weierstrass_invariants([omega1, omega3])` gives the invariants `[g2, g3]` of the lattice with half-periods `omega1`, `omega3`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# With `tau = omega3/omega1` reduced and `q = e^(im pi tau)`: `e1 = c (theta2^4 + 2 theta4^4)`, `e2 = c (theta2^4 - theta4^4)`, `e3 = -c (2 theta2^4 + theta4^4)`, `c = pi^2/(12 omega1^2)` (DLMF 23.6.2-4), then `g2 = 2 (e1^2 + e2^2 + e3^2)`, `g3 = 4 e1 e2 e3`.

#% item
# Exact half-periods with `omega3/omega1 = im` or `e^(im pi/3)` give closed forms.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
weierstrass_invariants([1.0, 2.0*im])
#% output
#= [8.124218443053017, 4.44305204494595]

#% cell
n(weierstrass_invariants([1, 2*im]), 30)
#% output
#= [8.124218443053017090717481882359900, 4.4430520449459499906285496780843793]

#% cell
weierstrass_invariants([1.5, 1.0 - im])
#% output
#= [-1.6758485436872796 + 4.365388350313805im, 2.469412539535982 - 1.3011730452487722im]

#% cell
weierstrass_invariants([1, im])
#% output
#= [gamma(1/4)^8/(256*pi^2), 0]

#% cell
weierstrass_invariants([1, exp(im*pi/3)])
#% output
#= [0, gamma(1/3)^18/(4096*pi^6)]

#% text
# Back to the half-periods:

#% cell
weierstrass_half_periods(weierstrass_invariants([1.0, 0.3 + 2*im]))
#% output
#= [1.0, 0.30000000000000315 + 1.999999999999999im]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `weierstrass_half_periods`, `weierstrass_e1`, `weierstrass_eta1`, `weierstrass_p`, `weierstrass_zeta`
